#speculation(title: [Histamine as Compensatory Arousal: H1 Blockade Unmasks Fatigue in a Hyperarousal Subtype])[
Histamine is a wake-promoting neuromodulator in the canonical arousal circuit @Jones2020ArousalCircuits. First-generation H1 antihistamines cross the blood-brain barrier and measurably impair vigilance, working memory, and speed @Kay2000AntihistamineCognition — the pharmacological signature of central H1 blockade removing a wakefulness drive. In mice, histamine (via histidine decarboxylase and H1) favours endurance and protects against exercise exhaustion, and H1 blockade or histamine deficiency impairs endurance @NiijimaYaoita2012HistamineFatigue.

This predicts a subtype-specific, not uniform, effect. In a hyperarousal ME/CFS subtype whose waking drive is partly maintained by histaminergic compensation, H1 blockade could remove that compensation and unmask fatigue; in patients without this compensatory drive, the same drug would do nothing or only sedate. The claim is deliberately split — it does not predict that antihistamines worsen all patients. The endurance evidence is mouse data, and the cognition evidence is from healthy adults given first-generation agents; neither establishes a hyperarousal ME/CFS subtype nor a human H1-blockade fatigue-unmasking effect (translation gap). (Certainty: 0.30)

_Falsifiable prediction:_ Pre-treatment markers of central histaminergic tone (for example cerebrospinal-fluid histamine, or waking maintenance on a sleep-latency test) identify a hyperarousal subtype in which a standardized H1-blockade challenge produces an objectively larger drop in vigilance or exercise endurance than in non-hyperaroused ME/CFS controls, and than in healthy controls.

*Consequence:* If correct, antihistamine choice in MCAS overlap would have to be subtype-matched — non-sedating second-generation agents for the hyperarousal subtype — and "antihistamines worsen fatigue" would be reinterpreted not as toxicity but as a diagnostic signal of compensatory histaminergic arousal.
] <spec:histamine-compensatory-arousal>
